# ESP32-Based Traffic Light Controller

## Project Overview
This project implements a traffic light controller using an ESP32 microcontroller and a finite state machine (FSM). It controls the sequence of traffic lights using predefined timing intervals and includes a pedestrian request mechanism.

The system uses a pedestrian push button to register crossing requests. Once a request is received, the controller completes the current traffic cycle and enters the pedestrian phase.

## Features
- Finite State Machine (FSM)-based traffic light control.
- Timed transitions between GREEN, YELLOW, and RED states.
- Pedestrian request handling using a push button.
- Button debouncing to reduce false triggering.
- Non-blocking timing using `millis()`.
- Serial Monitor messages for tracking state transitions.

## State Diagram

```mermaid
stateDiagram-v2
    [*] --> GREEN

    GREEN --> YELLOW: After 10 seconds
    YELLOW --> RED: After 3 seconds

    RED --> PEDESTRIAN: After 7 seconds / Request received
    RED --> GREEN: After 7 seconds / No request

    PEDESTRIAN --> GREEN: After 5 seconds

    note right of RED
        Vehicle red LED remains ON.
        Checks for pending pedestrian request.
    end note

    note right of PEDESTRIAN
        Vehicle red LED remains ON
        during the pedestrian phase.
    end note
```

## State Timing

| State | Duration | Action |
|---|---:|---|
| GREEN | 10 seconds | Green LED ON |
| YELLOW | 3 seconds | Yellow LED ON |
| RED | 7 seconds | Red LED ON |
| PEDESTRIAN | 5 seconds | Red LED remains ON |

A pedestrian request is stored when the button is pressed and is handled after the RED phase.

## Pin Configuration

| Component | ESP32 GPIO |
|---|---:|
| Red LED | GPIO 25 |
| Yellow LED | GPIO 26 |
| Green LED | GPIO 27 |
| Pedestrian Button | GPIO 14 |

The button uses the ESP32 internal pull-down configuration (`INPUT_PULLDOWN`).

## Software and Tools
- ESP32
- PlatformIO
- Arduino Framework
- C++

## Project Structure
- `src/main.cpp` – Main traffic light controller implementation.
- `platformio.ini` – PlatformIO project configuration.
- `include/` – Header files.
- `lib/` – Project libraries.
- `test/` – Test directory.

## Testing Status
The source code and project configuration are uploaded to GitHub. Physical hardware testing has not yet been performed.

## Future Improvements
- Hardware implementation and validation.
- Addition of pedestrian WALK/STOP indicators.
- Integration of vehicle detection sensors.
- Further testing of pedestrian request handling.
